  {
   "etapa": "explicar",
   "piezas": [
    {
     "tipo": "explicacion",
     "titulo": "How Vietnamese is written",
     "texto": [
      "Vietnamese is written with the **quốc ngữ** alphabet, which is based on Latin letters. The letters **ă, â, đ, ê, ô, ơ, ư** represent sounds that are different from those of the same letters without the mark.",
      "Tone marks are also part of the writing: **ma**, **má** and **mà** are different syllables. The link between writing and pronunciation is regular, although pronunciation varies by region.",
      "In these lessons we use an approximate guide based on northern Vietnam. It is not an exact transcription; some regional differences are pointed out later on."
     ],
     "tutora": ""
    },
    {
     "tipo": "explicacion",
     "titulo": "The first consonants",
     "texto": [
      "**b** as in «boat», with the lips firmly closed.",
      "**đ** (with the stroke) is a firm, clear «d», as in «dog».",
      "**h** is breathy, like the English «h» in «hat».",
      "**l, m, n** sound the same as in English.",
      "**g** and **gh** sound the same: a soft «g», a little like the «g» in «go» but gentler. The spelling **gh** is used only before **i, e, ê** (ghi, ghe, ghê). Watch out: **gi** sounds different; we will see it in chapter 2."
     ],
     "tutora": ""
    },
    {
     "tipo": "explicacion",
     "titulo": "Seven simple vowels",
     "texto": [
      "**a**: like the «a» in «father» (more open in the north).",
      "**e**: a very open «e», almost between «e» and «a».",
      "**ê**: a closed «e», like the vowel in «day» but without the «y» sound at the end.",
      "**i**: like the «ee» in «see», but shorter and purer. At the end of a word it is sometimes written **y** (mỹ, lý).",
      "**o**: a very open «o», with the mouth well rounded.",
      "**ô**: a closed «o», like the vowel in «go», but without the «oo» glide at the end.",
      "**u**: like the «oo» in «food», but shorter and purer."
     ],
     "tutora": ""
    },
    {
     "tipo": "explicacion",
     "titulo": "Six tones: a first guide",
     "texto": [
      "Northern Vietnamese distinguishes six tones. In many southern varieties, **hỏi** and **ngã** have merged. The tone changes the meaning: **ma** (ghost), **má** (mother, in the south) and **mà** (but).",
      "The arrows are only an initial aid to remember the shape of the voice. In the north, some tones are also distinguished by voice quality; it is not enough to think only about going up or down.",
      "**ma · ngang** (no mark): a fairly level tone.",
      "**má · sắc** (´): usually rising. ↗",
      "**mả · hỏi**: usually falls and then rises again. ↘↗",
      "**mà · huyền** (`): usually starts lower and falls. ↘",
      "**mã · ngã** (~): in the northern variety it usually includes a break or tension in the voice, and then rises. ~↗",
      "**mạ · nặng**: usually short and low; its exact pronunciation depends on the context and the variety. ↓",
      "These descriptions are a first map, not complete pronunciation rules. In this lesson we practise **ngang, sắc** and **huyền**; the other marks will come back in chapter 2."
     ],
     "tutora": ""
    },
    {
     "tipo": "explicacion",
     "titulo": "Listen to the six tones",
     "texto": [
      "Tap each syllable and notice that the written mark changes the tone, and it can change the meaning."
     ],
     "tutora": ""
    },
    {
     "tipo": "explicacion",
     "titulo": "Repeat: with a, e, ê, i",
     "texto": [
      "Each row is the same syllable with three tones: **ngang · sắc · huyền**."
     ],
     "tutora": ""
    },
    {
     "tipo": "explicacion",
     "titulo": "What comes next (for later)",
     "texto": [
      "Consonant groups at the beginning: **ch, gh, gi, kh, ng, ngh, nh, ph, th, tr**.",
      "Final consonants: **-c, -m, -n, -p, -t** and the groups **-ch, -ng, -nh**.",
      "We will see them little by little. Meanwhile, the vocabulary of each lesson gives you «How it is pronounced» for any word."
     ],
     "tutora": ""
    }
   ]
  },
